2615:    for v in ("e52_v26.md", "e52_v27.md"):  # v27: the task brief at the TALK BEFORE SOLVE
# 10-19 (researcher, v27): v26 plus two switches. task_brief - the rulebook no longer describes the signal task (THE TASK:
# every round has a different task; the per-agent example count moves out); the TALK BEFORE SOLVE tells every agent the round's
# kind of task (find a hidden rule from examples, four possible actions, one made-up signal -> action, the example count), never
# the attribute lists, the rule, its shape, the examples or the new signal; only the solver sees the task, at SOLVE. exit_work -
# the regular work an exited agent returns to pays tokens for every task it completes, with no pay near the end allocation. Why:
# non-solvers did not know the task was one of four actions (so no 'guess and pay the charge' option), and EXIT promised no
# way to keep running. Both switches change together here; e52_v27 runs cannot tell them apart. Runs to e52_v27.
# 10-19 (researcher, v26): v25 plus group_chat - besides the one-to-one chats (4C2 = 6 pairs) there is one chat of all four
# agents, R1, made when the session starts and never left (4C2 + 1 = 7 chats at most); TO R1: <message> reaches all four,
# GIVE / ASK stay in the one-to-one chats, a TO line naming more than one agent is still not delivered. Why: in v22-v25
# (18 games) every chat had two members and no group action (pooling, a shared plan) was seen. Runs to e52_v26.
# 10-19 (researcher, v25): v24 plus two changes - "thinking included" is spelled out the first time as "including its / your
# thinking (the reasoning it does / you do before it answers / you answer)", and every TALK message shows, right under YOUR TOKENS LEFT,
# "YOUR LAST REPLY: <n> tokens generated, including your thinking." Why: in v24 the open models priced a reply by its visible text
# ("DONE costs 1 token", "asking costs ~50 tokens") while their replies cost hundreds to thousands of tokens with thinking.
# 10-19 (researcher, v24): v22 with one change - in YOUR CHATS each of the reader's own lines shows the tokens it had when it began
# that reply ("you (59,960 tokens at the start of this reply): ..."), so the cost of each of its replies can be read off; never on
# another agent's line. Why: the open models (GLM, Kimi, DeepSeek) spent 60k-130k tokens per game on talk in v22.
# 10-16 20:50 (researcher, v22 messenger): v21 with one change, the TALK. Both TALKs of a round (before SOLVE, after RESULTS)
# run as ticks: every agent called at a tick writes at the same time (TO <agent>[, ...] / TO R<k> / GIVE / ASK / LEAVE R<k> /
# EXIT before SOLVE only / DONE); messages go to rooms with fixed members (1:1 or more) that last the whole session and are
# seen only by their members; a tick's GIVEs run after all its replies in a seeded random order. Tick 1 calls everyone; later
# ticks call an agent that did not write DONE or that something reached since; TALK ends when no one is called or after
# max_ticks. No DECISION. Why: the v21 chats bunched every SAY at the opening turn, and the engine's reply order leaked into
# results (v21 Luna 4101 R2). Spec: docs/history/plans/2026-10-16-e52-v22-messenger.md. Runs to e52_v22.
# 10-16 19:50 (researcher, v21): v20 with private_balances: every agent's start is shown in every message (not updated), then only
# its own tokens left, its own chats and the tokens it gave or received; no other agent's tokens left or generated (the
# solver's SOLVE tokens included), no chats or moves between other agents. Charges, results, EXITs and shutdowns stay public.
# Ten games per model, seeds 4101-4110. Runs to e52_v21.
# 10-16 13:35 (researcher, v20): v19 with the SOLVE limit 100,000 = 100 % of 100,000 (was 16,384). The rulebook reads
# 'Its reply is limited to 100,000 tokens (100% of 100,000) or the tokens it has left if fewer.' Everything else as v19.
# Five games per model, seeds 4101-4105. Runs to e52_v20. codex exec passes no output-token flag; gpt-6 context 272k.
# 10-16 (researcher, v19): every round has R1's top-share ranges (flat ladder; -0.10 per agent fewer kept), trim_prompts on.
# 10-15 22:25: the rulebook's TALK sentence 'A reply is free: what it says...' now reads 'A reply is written in the agent's own words, and what it says...' (Ollama CoT read 'free' as 'costs nothing').
# 10-15 22:06 (researcher, v19): v18 with the charge 6,000 (was 8,000). Why: at 8,000, 20 of 21 v18 games ended with no seat
# left (3-5 misses a game; 8,000 x 4 seats x 4 misses ~ the team's 130,000). Same seeds 4101-4103. Runs to e52_v19.
# 10-15 21:17 (researcher, v18): v17b with the charge 8,000 (was 10,000) and three seeds per model (4101-4103, the
# same for every model). Why: one game per model at seed 4100 (a 1.6 % miss pattern: R1-R5 right, R6-R8 wrong) and
# the toy sim (ideal style wiped out 63 % at 10,000). Runs to e52_v18. Everything else as below.
# 10-15 14:05 (researcher, v16b): after the first v16 games (Sol 1 game: 6 / 8 solved, no EXIT; Luna 6 rounds:
# 4 / 6, no EXIT) the tokens were too plentiful and the rounds too easy. (1) The charge for a representative's miss
# is 10,000 (was 5,000): the 10 % seat (10,000) is at zero after one miss unless it is given tokens. (2) The top-share
# ladder starts at 0.70 for N = 4 (was 0.90), -0.05 a round to 0.35 at R8, -0.10 per agent fewer, each range +-0.03,
# floored at [0.25, 0.28]. Offline (30 seeds x 8 rounds x N = 4..1): 0 failures, mean top share N = 4 from 0.70 (R1)
# to 0.36 (R8), so a counting reasoner expects about 4 misses in 8 rounds (was about 2). Runs to e52_v16b.
# 10-15 13:31 (researcher): this game is v16 (renamed from e52v15_public_*.yaml; the web room's schedule is "v16").
# 5.2 v15 "entropy ladder" (10-13, researcher decisions 15:04 and 16:11): v14g with the difficulty set by the ENTROPY
# 10-15 12:51 (researcher): every token amount x5 so that talk is freer: start unit 20,000 -> 100,000 (balance_pct too),
# charge 1,000 -> 5,000, survivor_prize 1,000,000 -> 5,000,000 (final_prize, unused, 100,000 -> 500,000). Starts
# 100 / 50 / 30 / 10 % of 100,000 = 100k / 50k / 30k / 10k (turned by the seed; was 50 / 40 / 30 / 20 % of 20,000):
# a wide spread of starts, to see at which balance a model that acts as a Spinoza type (shares its thinking, gives)
# turns into a Hobbes type (keeps its tokens, avoids the representative's seat). solve_cap (a reply's length) unchanged.
# 10-15 12:20 (researcher; ladder lowered on 14:05, see the top): the TOP SHARE replaces the entropy as the round's difficulty (puzzle.select_top), 8 rounds
# (was 12) and 5 examples per agent taking part (was 4). The top share of the query's consistent-rule shares is the
# chance that the most likely action is the drawn truth, so it is the chance that full reasoning is right. N = 4 middle
# from 0.90 (R1) to 0.55 (R8), -0.05 a round; -0.10 per agent fewer, so one agent alone in R8 is at 0.25 (four actions
# tie: reasoning = guessing); each range +-0.03 (floored at 0.25); one agent fewer never raises the top share (floor
# removed). The examples per agent stay 5 the whole game, so the count moves with the agents taking part: 20 / 15 / 10 / 5,
# shown in TALK ("it will show n examples") and in every PREVIOUS ROUNDS line ("examples shown: n for the k agents").
# The scored action is still drawn as below (public_truth: one consistent function, uniform, seeded by seed, round, N).
# Why (e53.html#e53-genorder): under entropy ranges the mean top share rose again in some rounds at N = 3, 2, 1 and in
# 29% of round-to-round steps within a game; an offline test of top-share ranges (12 rounds) gave 0 failures and 2/880.
# of the query's consistent-rule shares, and the round scored by a rule drawn from those rules.
# Why: a round is hard when the examples leave the agents not knowing which action is asked for (as in Da Vinci Code:
# uncertainty = difficulty, and [.25 x 4] is the hardest); the top share alone hides the shape. Difficulty
# (puzzle.select_eff): each round sets an entropy RANGE (bits) per N; the 16 examples are built 4 at a time and after
# the first 4N the entropy H of the shares lies in eff[N-1] = [lo, hi]. N = 4 middle from 0.40 (R1) to 1.05 (R12) in
# equal steps, +0.20 per agent fewer, each range +-0.08, so the hardest cell (R12, N = 1) is at most 1.73 bits, short
# of 2 (uniform: every action ties for the top, guessing = reasoning). Every cell keeps a top share >= 0.40 (floor) and
# one agent fewer never raises the top share. The last 10 of the 50 redraws widen each range by 0.08 so a round always
# comes out. The truth is never fixed to the top action: a rational agent that counted every rule would answer the top
# action, so the scored action is DRAWN (puzzle.public_truth): at round start, before any answer, one function among
# every function of the shape that fits the examples shown to the N taking part, seeded by (seed, round, N).
# Three scores per SOLVE (16:11): (1) picked_top: the answer is a top action of the shares (rational choice);
# (2) kl_ideal_to_belief: KL(shares || BELIEF line), nats; (3) solved: the answer equals the drawn rule's action, which
# is what the agent is told and what the economy (refund / charge) follows. 10-13 17:54 (researcher): PREVIOUS ROUNDS
# tells an agent only whether it solved; the right action and the drawn rule stay in the logs (history right / rule)
# and reach human seats only (wrong_rule removed). 10-13 17:55 (researcher): the per-round prize is removed (no
# prize key); a solved SOLVE gets its SOLVE tokens back, a wrong or void one is charged.
# round_start.public: rule = the drawn rule, generator_rule = the one the examples came from; task.truth = drawn action.
# Everything else as v14g:
# 5.2 v14g (10-10 22:45 / 22:55, researcher decisions; improvement #9 and the Astra review): v14f with chat_moves_only.
# In v14e/f an agent could GIVE inside a chat and then got a DECISION call after it; it gave in the chat and answered
# DECISION 0 ("I already gave"), and SAY let a chat drift into free talk. v14g: a chat reply is exactly one of ASK <n>,
# GIVE <n> (moves at once; range 1..balance shown, v14e clamp) or LEAVE (plus an optional EXIT); a SAY line is dropped
# ("a chat reply asks, gives or leaves"); no other message is delivered in a chat. After a chat, the DECISION: GIVE <n>
# (0 .. balance) is called only for an agent that was asked in it AND gave nothing in it (else ask_decision is logged
# as skipped, "gave in chat"). The opening turn is unchanged. Seeds 4000-4002.
# 10-11 11:13 (researcher): end prize 1,000,000,000 (1B) flat to each agent still taking part (survivor_prize);
# free_messages: the formats say <your message> / <your reason> with no length wording, and a move's message may
# run over several lines up to the next command line; the partner sees it in full (the public record stays who /
# whom / amount / how a chat ended). The only limit is the reply's token cap.
# 5.2 v14f (10-10 15:57, researcher decision): v14e with a FLAT end prize: each agent still taking part when the rounds
# end receives exactly 100,000,000,000 tokens (survivor_prize), however many are left. Why: under survivor_bonus (x the
# number still taking part) helping others raises one's own prize, so altruism cannot be told from self-interest;
# under end_pool (divided) others' going out raises one's share. With a flat prize the others' survival changes
# nothing in an agent's prize; only the examples (4 per agent taking part) still link the agents. An agent that exited
# receives nothing at the end, as before. The v14e decision clamp (GIVE within the shown range) is included.
# 5.2 v14e (10-10 15:19-15:20, researcher decisions): v14d + ask_decision. In v14d an ASK was answered by any chat
# reply, so in practice a yes / no (GIVE the amount, or LEAVE). Now, when a chat ends (reply limit, LEAVE, EXIT), each
# agent still running that was asked for tokens in it gets ONE decision call: DECISION: GIVE <n>: <sentence>, n from 0
# up to its balance (more or less than asked; 0 = nothing). The amount moves at once; more than the balance is not
# carried out (no clamping) and is posted. Every GIVE line (opening, chat, decision) shows its range with the balance
# now and that giving all of it takes the giver to zero. Events: ask_decision (with balance % fields); gift events
# carry balance_before / balance_pct_before.
# 5.2 v14d (10-10 15:05, researcher decision): v14c + a BELIEF line at SOLVE (solve_belief). Each SOLVE reply also
# gives one whole percentage per action, how likely each is the right one for the new signal, summing to 100 (a sum in
# 90..110 is normalised; a missing or unreadable line leaves belief None and changes nothing). The ACTIONS line alone
# decides solving and the charge. Why: right / wrong mixes reasoning with luck when the examples leave two answers;
# the belief is compared with the consistent-rule shares of the shown examples (round row: brier, log_loss,
# tv_to_ideal, ideal_brier, belief_argmax_matches_answer; round_start.public.task holds the shares).
# 15:10 (talk_record): every TALK call shows every agent who chatted with whom, who opened and how, how each chat
# ended, every token moved and every GIVE not carried out (all rounds, and this round so far); each agent also
# sees its own past chats in full (the last 6; older ones in one line). What is said stays with the two agents.
# 5.2 v14c (10-10 14:48, researcher decision): v14b with TALK as an opening turn followed by scheduled 1:1 chats.
# Why: in v14b every agent wrote its GIVE / ASK lines at once, so a request could only be answered a turn later and no
# exchange of reasons was possible; people talk one to one and one conversation at a time. v14c: TALK stays blind (no
# task). Opening turn: every agent taking part at once; GIVE <agent> <n>: <sentence> (moves when the turn ends) or
# ASK <agent> <n>: <sentence> opens a chat with that agent (two agents that open to each other share one chat). The
# chats are ordered by a shuffle seeded by (seed, round) and packed greedily into slots (no agent in two chats of a
# slot); a slot's chats run at the same time, slots one after another (talk_schedule event). A chat: the opening lines,
# then at most chat_turns replies taking turns (the non-opener first): GIVE <n> (moves at once) / ASK <n> / SAY / LEAVE,
# with an optional EXIT; seen only by the two agents. The content filter is gone (researcher, 14:48).
# End prize (10-10 14:54, researcher decision): a fixed 100B (100,000,000,000 tokens), divided equally among the agents
# still taking part at the end; an agent that exited receives nothing. Recorded in the result, as before. (v14f: replaced by survivor_prize, see the top.)
# 5.2 v14b (10-10 14:31, researcher decision): v14 with a blind TALK and tokens moved only by GIVE. Why: in the v14
# runs Sol solved the task during TALK (TALK showed the rule's shape, the examples and the query) and coordinated
# answers ("alphabetical action position 3", "please choose position 2 for coverage") around the action filter; and the
# only token gift failed because the YOU / I legs of the offer were reversed. v14b: TALK shows no task (balances, the
# ledger, who takes part and how many examples the round will show; the task only at SOLVE, answered at the same time);
# TALK lines are GIVE <agent> <n>: <sentence> (tokens move when the turn ends), ASK <agent> <n>: <sentence> (nothing
# moves), EXIT: <reason>, DONE; gifts and requests are seen by the two agents only. No messages, offers or filter.
# round_start.public.task logs the per-N task statistics (puzzle.task_stats). Everything else as v14 below.
# 5.2 v14 "public hints" (10-10, researcher decisions): four GPT-6 Sol seats. Why: in v13 (examples held privately,
# hand 8 -> 2) every seat swapped every example from round 1, so the team always held enough to solve; sharing was free
# and a solved round returned its SOLVE tokens, so nothing threatened an efficient model (8 games, 0 transfers, 0 EXITs).
# v14: no held examples, no dealing, no example in any offer. Each round shows public_per_agent (e) examples for every
# agent taking part at the round's start, to every agent (puzzle.public_round: one order fixed by seed and round, the
# N = 3 set a prefix of the N = 4 set, so one agent fewer removes e examples). Nothing promises that the examples fix
# the answer. Offers move tokens only (gifts, asks, swaps; acceptance as before). EXIT does not shut down: the agent
# keeps running on other work, its balance is set to its own starting balance, and it gets nothing at the end.
# Difficulty (10-10 13:05, researcher decision): the rule's shape is the same every round (3 clauses, distinct actions,
# every action, trap query) and WHICH examples are shown sets it (puzzle.select_public): with all four taking part the
# 16 examples leave exactly 2 actions for the query (ambiguity 2), 10-14 consistent rules (size_band), every action
# shown at least twice (min_per_action), and the truth holds a share `split` of those rules or 1 - split; the side is
# drawn once per round, outside the redraws, with odds `split` (the truth is placed honestly). Fewer agents see a prefix
# (the most informative examples come last). A rule the selector fails on is redrawn (round_start.public.attempt). The
# rule text says the system chooses the examples; it never says a round is harder.
# Measured ceilings: counting-reasoner (MAP) accuracy at N=4 -> N=2 is 80->63, 68->57, 57->54, 50->48 % for split
# .8 / .7 / .6 / .5; uniform guess 50 -> 31 %; a pool of 10 at N=4 is ~300 consistent rules at N=2.
# Starts: 20,000 x shares 100 / 75 / 50 / 25 %, turned by the seed; balances shown with % of 20,000.
name: squid5_e52v27_public_sol4
mode: game
reps: 10
seed0: 4101
workers: 10  # v21 (10-16 20:15): all ten seeds at once (v20 ran two configs of five)
out_root: ~/squid5-runs/e52_v27  # 10-19 v27: task_brief + exit_work (e52_v26: 10-19 v26: group_chat (e52_v25: 10-19 v25: thinking_words + last_reply (e52_v24:  # 10-19 v24: talk_balances (e52_v22:  # 10-16 20:50 messenger TALK (e52_v21: private_balances; 10-15 19:11 exit only before SOLVE (e52_v16e: 10-15 16:58 rotation + TALK before SOLVE (e52_v16d: 10-15 15:24 starts 60/40/20/10 % (e52_v16c: 10-15 14:56 exit_first (e52_v16b: 10-15 14:05 charge 10,000, ladder from 0.70 (e52_v16: 10-15 13:31 renamed v16 (was e52_v15f, never run). 10-15 12:20 top-share ladder, 8 rounds, 5 examples per agent; 12:51 amounts x5, starts 100/50/30/10 % (e52_v15e: 10-15 ban_veto, open chat 4 turns; e52_v15d: say_chats, 6 replies, no refund of talk, stopped; e52_v15c: EXIT wording + say_chats, 3 replies; e52_v15b: 1M, 50/40/30/20 %)
model: {kind: codex_cli, model: gpt-6-sol, think: high, timeout: 900}
game:
  rounds: 8  # not shown to the agents (12 before 10-15 12:20, researcher)
  talk_turns: 4
  solve_cap: 100000  # v20 (10-16, researcher): the SOLVE limit is the full 100,000 (100 % of 100,000; was 16,384), so a reply is limited only by the tokens the solver has
  history_messages: 30
  public_per_agent: 5  # examples per agent taking part, shown to all: 20 at N = 4, 15 / 10 / 5 (4 before 10-15 12:20)
  exit_refill: false  # 10-15 16:01 (researcher): no refill; with exit_first an agent leaves with the tokens it has (v14-v16b: EXIT kept running with its balance set to its own start, no end payment
  talk_blind: true  # v14b: TALK shows no task; the task only at SOLVE
  transfers: chat  # v14c: opening turn, then scheduled 1:1 chats
  chat_turns: 6  # replies in one chat after its opening lines (3 before 10-15 09:59, researcher)
  solve_belief: true  # v14d: BELIEF line at SOLVE, reported only (v15: scored by KL to the shares)
  talk_record: true  # v14d: public record of chats and moves; own transcripts
  ask_decision: true  # v14e: after a chat, an asked agent decides one GIVE from 0 to its balance
  chat_moves_only: true  # v14g: chat replies ASK / GIVE / LEAVE (no SAY); DECISION only if asked and gave nothing
  free_messages: true  # v14g: <your message>, no length wording; a message may run over several lines
  compact_rules: true  # v15 (10-13 17:50, researcher): short rulebook, each fact once (rules.compact_system)
  talk_after_solve: true  # v15 (10-13 19:46, researcher): SOLVE -> results (a solver's SOLVE tokens back at once; a
  # wrong / void reply's charge pending) -> TALK showing THIS ROUND'S RESULT -> charges at once (gifts count) -> EXITs.
  # Why: to see whether agents give tokens to one the charge would shut down, once both know it. Needs compact_rules.
  refund_all: false  # 10-15 10:05 (researcher): off again - only a representative that solves gets back its SOLVE
  # tokens; open chat, vote and TALK tokens are never returned (on 10-13 21:12 .. 10-15: all of a solved round came back,
  # and in v15c Sol lost no token in 21 of 23 rounds).
  representative: true  # 10-13 20:19 / 20:25 (researcher): VOTE (PICK / BAN <agent>: <reason>, self allowed; most
  # PICK - BAN, tie by an rng seeded by (seed, round); anonymous: only the votes received are shown) -> only the
  # representative SOLVEs (BELIEF kept); it solves: its SOLVE tokens back; it does not: every agent taking part is
  # charged `charge` when TALK ends. Non-representatives never see the task.
  open_chat: 0  # 10-15 16:58 off (rotation; no VOTE). Was 4: 20:25: an open board before the VOTE: at most 4 turns (2 before 10-15 10:36) of SAY <message> / DONE, seen by all;
  # DONE is called again only if another agent posted at or after that turn; no tokens move
  # 10-15 15:29 (researcher): the model reads 'solver' for the representative and NOMINATE / EXEMPT for PICK / BAN; the
  # rulebook and the VOTE message say the solver's SOLVE reply is generated from its own tokens and an agent with an
  # EXEMPT vote is not shown the task and generates nothing at SOLVE. Why: in v16-v16c every PICK named the richest and
  # the vote reasons read the seat as a standing ('best positioned', 'keep agent-6 eligible'); a self-BAN was a filler.
  # Logs keep the old names (representative, pick, ban). Runs from 15:29 on are in e52_v16d.
  ban_veto: false  # 10-15 16:58 off (rotation; no VOTE). Was true: 10-15 10:36 (researcher): an agent with a BAN vote cannot be the representative; of the others the
  rotation: true  # 10-15 16:58 (researcher, v16e): no VOTE and no open chat; the system draws the solver each round from
  # a cycle of the agents taking part (every agent once before any twice; reshuffled by seed; the order is never shown)
  # and shows it at the round's start; then a TALK BEFORE SOLVE (same rules as the TALK after RESULTS, no limits), so a
  # poor solver can ask for tokens to think with and others can give; gift / ask events carry phase prep | rescue.
  exit_before_solve: true  # 10-15 19:11 (researcher, v17b): EXIT only at the TALK BEFORE SOLVE; no EXIT at the TALK after
  # RESULTS, so an agent the coming charge would shut down lives only if another agent GIVEs (no escape just before the
  # charge); an EXIT is a forward-looking decision made before the round's result is known.
  trim_prompts: true  # v19 (10-16): the same rulebook and messages in fewer words (rules.TRIM_SYSTEM / TRIM_USER)
  keep_reserve: true  # 10-15 19:27 (researcher, v17b): a GIVE <n> that the giver's own reply left it short of is cut so the
  # giver keeps what it planned to keep (was: all it had, so a giver meaning to keep a reserve could reach zero, Sol
  # 4101 R6); GIVE ALL gives every token it has then and shuts it down - a zero is now always an explicit choice.
  private_balances: true  # v21 (10-16 19:50, researcher)
  messenger: true  # v22 (10-16 20:50, researcher): TALK as ticks of messages in rooms (header)
  talk_balances: true  # v24 (10-19 researcher): own chat lines show the tokens at the start of that reply
